**Torch Backend:**
> no defined batch size

Defining a batch size in the dataset `__init__` or in the call to `model.fit()` does not fix the issue. See the code example below.

> need to define `__len__`

Further, defining `__len__` (which serves the same purpose as `num_batches` since #19624) also has no effect, neither for a finite dataset (len = some integer) nor for an infinite one (len = None). See the code example below.

> use `.repeat()`

I'm not sure what object you want me to call this on. The dataset does not have a `.repeat()` method under torch backend, and the data itself is already in batched format as per my original code example.

**TensorFlow Backend:**

> need to define the size of data points and the batch size

Just like in torch backend, this has no effect on the warning or the behavior.

**JAX Backend:**

> Automatic Parallelization

I'm admittedly not super familiar with JAX yet. However, the idea behind using multiprocessing in the dataset is to use the CPU to pre-fetch data before a neural network needs it, thus avoiding bottlenecks in training. We use multiprocessing to gain parallelization benefits even in cases where the data fetching process is CPU bound (rather than I/O bound), which is often the case when extensive pre-processing is applied to the data (think augmentation, latent point sampling, or mini-batch optimal transport).

In that sense, I do not think simply not using multiprocessing when using JAX backend is a solution, as this makes us CPU bound again. Whether or not the data generating process is internally parallelized (automatically by JAX or by some other means) does not matter.

